op and tall from the bottom.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has one background color and one foreground color; the foreground forms\nseparate solid rectangles, ordered from left to right.\n\nRule\nBuild a 10-row background canvas whose width is the packed width of the wide\nversions of the rectangles. For each source rectangle, paint a top-aligned\nrectangle of shape min(h,w) by max(h,w), with one background column between\nconsecutive rectangles. Then, starting again from the left edge, paint a\nbottom-aligned rectangle of shape max(h,w) by min(h,w) for each source rectangle,\nagain separated by one background column.\n\nKey insight\nThe input rectangles are not copied in their original orientations; each object\ncontributes one horizontal-facing and one vertical-facing normalized rectangle,\npreserving left-to-right object order.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"solid monochromatic foreground rectangles ordered left-to-right\",\n  \"dst\": \"packed upper wide slots and lower tall slots in the same left-to-right order\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"rank(s) == rank(d) for both the upper and lower slot sequences\",\n  \"transform\": \"paint each rectangle twice: min(h,w) by max(h,w) top-aligned in the upper band, and max(h,w) by min(h,w) bottom-aligned in the lower band, with one background column between slots\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"height 10; width is the sum of max(h,w) over source rectangles plus one separator column between consecutive rectangles\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2601afb7\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3644\nmechanism=0.4435\nlexical=0.0138\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:rotation, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:rotate, operation:sort_order, relation:aligned, relation:direction\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/2601afb7.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/2601afb7.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRotate each vertical bar's color one slot right and its height one slot left.\n\nInput structure\n- A dominant background surrounds several monochrome one-cell-wide vertical bars.\n- Every bar is bottom-aligned and occupies a distinct column.\n- The ordered bar slots are read from left to right.\n\nRule\n- Keep the grid shape and the bar columns unchanged.\n- At slot i, use the color from the previous slot cyclically.\n- At slot i, use the height from the next slot cyclically.\n\nKey insight\n- Color and height are independent cyclic attributes of the ordered bar slots:\n  colors rotate right while heights rotate left.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"bottom-aligned one-cell-wide vertical bars ordered left-to-right\",\n  \"transform\": \"redraw each bar in the same column with the previous bar's color cyclically and the next bar's height cyclically\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"83b6b474\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3576\nmechanism=0.4717\nlexical=0.0128\ncode_feature=0.2857\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:rotate, operation:sort_order, operation:translate, relation:between\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/83b6b474.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/83b6b474.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nTranslate the colored objects into the most balanced complete rectangular frame.\n\nInput structure\nA grid with one dominant background color and several monochromatic\n4-connected foreground objects that are straight bars or corner-like line\nsprites.\n\nRule\nPreserve every foreground object exactly, allowing translation only, and\nplace the objects so they cover every pixel of a one-cell-thick rectangular\nframe exactly once; the frame interior is the background color.\n\nKey insight\nThe output shape is not copied from the input: the foreground pixel count\ndetermines possible frame sizes, and the intended output is the valid frame\nassembly with the least difference between height and width.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"monochromatic 4-connected line sprite s\",\n  \"dst\": \"frame-border slot d in a complete one-cell-thick rectangular frame\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.shape and s.orientation fit d without rotation; assigned slots cover every border pixel exactly once\",\n  \"transform\": \"translate s into d; fill the frame interior with the background color\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"most balanced rectangle whose border pixel count equals the total foreground pixel count\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"62593bfd\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3619\nmechanism=0.4107\nlexical=0.0129\ncode_feature=0.6667\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:counter, code:rotation, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:rotate, operation:sort_order, operation:translate, relation:direction\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/62593bfd.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/62593bfd.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSort objects to top or bottom edge by whether their shape (up to rotation/flip) is shared.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several multicolor connected components (8-connected) scattered across the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Compute each object's canonical shape (binary mask, invariant to rotation and flip).\n- Objects whose shape appears in two or more components → translate to TOP edge (row 0).\n- Objects whose shape is unique → translate to BOTTOM edge (row H−h).\n- Horizontal position (left column) is preserved; only vertical translation changes.\n\nKey insight: The sorting criterion is shape frequency (rotation-invariant), not area — duplicated shapes go up, unique shapes go down.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"all connected components (objects) on the background\",\n  \"dst\": \"translate each object d vertically to an edge (x preserved)\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"group objects by their pixel-count (area). If an area value occurs >=2 times, those objects go to the TOP edge; if the area is unique, that object goes to the BOTTOM edge\",\n  \"transform\": \"duplicated-size -> top; singleton-size -> bottom\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"c7f57c3e\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3431\nmechanism=0.4240\nlexical=0.0166\ncode_feature=0.4286\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:rotation, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:translate, relation:aligned, relation:between, relation:direction, relation:overlap\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/c7f57c3e.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/c7f57c3e.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSwap every sprite to the opposite prototype while preserving scale and position.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Two distinct small sprite patterns (prototypes) appear at various scales.\n- Each sprite instance is a scaled copy of one of the two prototypes, placed on the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the two prototype patterns from the smallest-area sprites.\n- For each sprite, determine which prototype it matches and its integer scale factor.\n- Replace it with the other prototype at the same scale, aligned by an internal anchor region (color-1 pixels).\n\nKey insight: Every sprite toggles to the alternate prototype; the anchor sub-region inside each sprite pins the alignment so swapped sprites land in the correct position regardless of scale.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
